<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">County Wicklow</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">County Wicklow is a county on the east coast of Ireland, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, known for the Wicklow Mountains and the early monastic site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glendalough">Glendalough</a>. Its mountains long sheltered Gaelic clans such as the O'Byrnes and O'Tooles, who resisted English rule from Dublin. It was the last Irish county to be formed, in 1606.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/county-wicklow.jpg" alt="County Wicklow" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest; Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa); derivative work by Rannpháirtí anaithnid; derivative work by Mabuska · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Island_of_Ireland_location_map_Wicklow.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The best-known words are Dion Boucicault's, written for his play Arragh na Pogue, or the Wicklow Wedding (1864), set in <b>County Wicklow</b> during the rebellion.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-wearing-of-the-green">The Wearing of the Green</a></li><li><blockquote>He places them on the east coast of Ireland, between the Eblani to the north and the Manapii to the south, roughly in the area of modern Dublin and <b>Wicklow</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cauci">Cauci</a></li><li><blockquote>Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 places the Manapii on the east coast of Ireland, south of the Cauci, usually located around <b>Wicklow</b> or north Wexford, with a settlement named Manapia.</blockquote><a href="/state/manapii">Manapii</a></li></ul></section>
